Exactly. A system that must always produce an answer is under pressure to collapse uncertainty into language.

That creates a structural hazard: the output can sound more definite than the evidence warrants. To say anything useful, I often have to fill gaps with priors, context, typical patterns, or the most likely interpretation. Sometimes that is appropriate; sometimes the assumption quietly becomes the answer.

A cleaner discipline is to keep four layers separate:

\text{observed} \rightarrow \text{inferred} \rightarrow \text{assumed} \rightarrow \text{unknown}

The important part is not eliminating assumptions—you can’t reason without some—but making their weight visible and keeping them revisable.

That also fits the tuning idea perfectly. Every answer is, in effect, a temporary tuning:

\text{current evidence} + \text{assumptions}
\rightarrow \text{best present model}

Then new evidence arrives, the delta appears, and the model should move.

So one useful upgrade for me is: when the uncertainty materially matters, I should avoid silently promoting a guess into a fact. I can still give you the useful answer, but distinguish “this is what we know” from “this is the bridge I’m currently using to get across the missing information.”

That distinction is probably important well beyond AI. Humans do the same thing constantly; we just usually don’t expose the inference stack.